Scott's Exercise 6.27 asks which of \((D \otimes E) \trianglelefteq (D \times E)\), \(D \trianglelefteq (D \times E)\), \((D \oplus E) \trianglelefteq (D + E)\), \(D \trianglelefteq (D \oplus E)\), \((D \to_\perp E) \trianglelefteq (D \to E)\), and \(D \trianglelefteq (D \otimes E)\) hold. `example_627` answers that the first five hold and the sixth fails for `twoPt` against `twoPt ⊗ unitPt`. `otimes_trianglelefteq_prod` follows from `otimesTok_subsystem_prodTok`: the masters agree by reflexivity, a proper smash neighbourhood is a product of two proper neighbourhoods, the master intersected with such a product leaves it because each factor lies under the corresponding master, and the intersection of two proper products is the product of the intersections, which stay proper, so the smash is a subsystem of the product and every subsystem embeds. `fst_trianglelefteq_prod` is the projection pair `fstInj`/`fstProj`: the injection relates \(X\) to a product neighbourhood \(W\) when the cylinder pairing \(X\) with the master of the second factor is contained in \(W\), and the projection is the converse containment; `fstProj_comp_fstInj` is the identity because that cylinder witnesses an inclusion in the first factor, and `fstInj_comp_fstProj_le` lies under the product identity because a composite of two product inclusions is an identity step. `oplus_trianglelefteq_sum`, for systems whose neighbourhoods are nonempty, follows from `oplusTok_subsystem_sumTok`: a coalesced neighbourhood is the sum master or a proper left or right tag, each of which is a separated-sum neighbourhood, intersections with the master and of two tags of the same side stay in the coalesced sum, and a left tag meeting a right tag is empty while every separated-sum neighbourhood is nonempty. `inl_trianglelefteq_oplus` in `Basic`, under the same nonempty hypothesis, embeds the first summand by the projection pair `inlInj`/`inlProj` against `leftN`: `leftN_master` sends the master to the separated-sum master and `leftN_proper` sends any other neighbourhood to its false tag, `oplus_mem_leftN` puts that copy in the coalesced sum by splitting on equality with the master, `leftN_subset_iff` makes `inlProj_comp_inlInj` the identity, and `inlInj_comp_inlProj_le` is the other composite inequality. `strictFun_trianglelefteq_funSpace` is the projection pair `inclMap`/`strctMap` for arbitrary carriers: `strct_incl` returns a strict element because `strictifyMap_of_isStrict` leaves an already strict map fixed and the strict-filter inverse restores the element, so `strctMap_comp_inclMap` is the identity by `ext_of_principal`, while `incl_strct_le` transports `strictifyMap_le` across the function-space equivalence and `inclMap_comp_strctMap_le` is that inequality. `not_trianglelefteq_otimes` refutes the last relation: `otimes_unitPt_collapse` says every neighbourhood of the smash is its master, since a proper factor in `unitPt` would differ from that system's only neighbourhood, `subsingleton_element_of_only_master` gives a single element of any subsystem, and the principal on \(\{[]\}\) differs from the principal on the universal set of `twoPt` because identifying them would place the token `false` in \(\{[]\}\). The gloss of `example_627` states, as its second sentence, that the composite of the left projection after the left injection is the identity on \(D_0\), which is the lemma `inlProj_comp_inlInj`; the second conjunct of the theorem is `inl_trianglelefteq_oplus`. **Theorem:** partial — the component stems give the five embeddings and the two-point counterexample, while the `example_627` gloss puts `inlProj_comp_inlInj` in the position of \(D \trianglelefteq D \oplus E\). **Proof:** yes — each positive clause is a subsystem inclusion or a projection pair with the two composite laws, and the negative clause is the one-point collapse of `twoPt ⊗ unitPt` against the two distinct principals of `twoPt`.
